11. SECURITY RESEARCH AND RESPONSIBLE DISCLOSURE
================================================================

Good-faith technical review of publicly available
materials is welcome.

Security researchers must not interpret public
documentation as permission to access or test
private infrastructure.

Any security testing of nonpublic systems requires
explicit authorization from the appropriate owner.

Reports should identify, where possible:

- the affected public component or specification;
- the relevant version or commit;
- the expected behavior;
- the observed behavior;
- reproduction steps;
- supporting technical evidence;
- the potential security impact.

Do not submit credentials, private keys, access tokens,
confidential customer information, or regulated data
without an appropriate authorized process.

Security concerns may be directed to the project
contact listed below.

